    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "Hi @MurrayData, thanks for the report. First, I think you should update your installation of cuDF. I noticed you still have the negative floating point CSV problem, but that is fixed in the latest. Also, you refer to pygdf, which is out of date, so I'm wondering if you are using a \"very old\" version (where \"Very old\" is more than a month. \ud83d\ude04 )\r\n\r\nI think this is not a bug, it's just pandas rounding the displayed value to the number of digits read in from the file. If you display the dataframe, you see the rounded version. If you just print a value, you will see the full precision. e.g. `print(df['lat'][0])` prints `54.60244399999999`, where `df` is a *pandas* dataframe, not a cuDF.\r\n\r\nAlso, you may be interested in this code, which is pure Python, no pandas or cuDF:\r\n```python\r\nx = 54.602444\r\nprint(x)\r\nprint('%.16f' % x)\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput: \r\n```\r\n54.602444\r\n54.6024439999999984\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe image below shows my full experiment, which demonstrates that Python, pandas, and cuDF all store the same value for this input.\r\n\r\nIf there's something to enhance, it's the behavior of printing cuDF DataFrames. We could look at what pandas does for rounding for display.\r\n\r\nAs I mentioned previously, most decimal values are not exactly representable in finite-precision binary floating point. Specifically, only values where the denominator is a power of 2 are exactly representable. By way of exploration, this website lets you enter a decimal value and see the floating point value, and it will tell you whether or not the value is exact: https://www.exploringbinary.com/floating-point-converter/\r\n\r\n<img width=\"592\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/783069/49129606-875a1480-f324-11e8-88cb-4da582391276.png\">",
